WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.710
- After my Business Bachelor

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:03.190
at the University of Munich,

00:00:03.190 --> 00:00:04.990
I completed a Finance Master

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:06.750
at the London School of Economics.

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:09.840
Later, I completed an MBA at INSEAD.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.360
And for both of these programs,
I needed a TOEFL test.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.020
And I did not just want any result,

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.270
but of course I wanted a higher result,

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:20.140
because I knew that these
places were competitive

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:22.250
and that I needed to perform well

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:24.370
in order to have a good chance to get in.

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:27.670
And indeed, I was able to on
the first test for the LSE,

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:30.700
to score 113 on the TOEFL iBT,

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:34.460
and then later I even
scored 117 on the TOEFL iBT

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:37.860
that was the second test that
I used for my INSEAD MBA.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.620
A couple of months ago,
I did a video on the GMAT

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.180
where I showed you my test-taking strategy

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.930
and how I achieve to score above 700

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:48.780
in three weeks preparation
time for the GMAT.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.180
This video was very well
received by many viewers.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.190
So I wanted to create a
similar video on the TOEFL,

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:55.060
if you're interested only

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:57.420
in specific sections of this video.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.870
For instance, if you want to
directly jump to the section

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.260
where I share my test-taking strategies,

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:04.780
I included some chapters
with the timestamps below

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:05.840
in the video description.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.110
So have a looked there and
then you can directly skip

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:09.840
to the part that you're
most interested in.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.190
So welcome everyone to
another coffee break

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:14.340
here on my channel Firm Learning.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.200
My name is Heinrich and on my channel

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.950
I want to help you to succeed

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:20.100
in the first years of your career.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.620
So again, today it's quite
late here in Germany.

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:24.780
So I will help my myself to a bit of water

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:26.480
but you please, get a coffee.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.750
Let's have a great coffee
break together you guys.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.210
So I will structured
the video in four parts.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:33.770
First, I'm going to talk about
what the TOEFL actually is.

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:35.570
Then I'm going to show some reflections

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:38.280
on what score you will
need for the top programs.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.540
Then I'm going to
briefly compare the TOEFL

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:43.140
to its biggest competitor, the IELTS test

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:44.880
and give you some hints how to decide

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.750
whether you should take
the one or the other.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:48.110
And then finally, I'm going to share

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:49.750
my test-taking strategies,

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:51.510
the tips and tricks that I have

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:52.470
that I believe well help

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.460
to really score high on the test.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:55.570
So let's start it off.

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:57.060
What is the TOEFL test?

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:00.570
And TOEFL stands for Test of
English as a Foreign Language.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:02.910
And indeed if you're not
a native English speaker

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:05.190
and want to study abroad at a university

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:07.340
where English is the main language.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:10.110
These universities will
often ask you for proof

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:12.780
that you actually speak
English at a good level.

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:16.350
And TOEFL is one of the tests
that is most widely accepted

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:17.800
in the university world.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.070
More than 11,000 universities

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:24.270
and over 150 countries
accept the TOEFL test.

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:27.380
At least this is what its
creator, ETS is claiming

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:28.260
on its webpage.

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:30.980
Therefore, the TOEFL is
designed to test exactly this

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:32.930
namely your ability to cope

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:36.040
in an English speaking
university classroom

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:37.930
whether you're able to contribute

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:40.490
to these types of
discussions at university.

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:42.730
Specifically, the test
consists of four parts,

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:45.230
reading, speaking, listening and writing.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:46.480
For all of these questions,

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:47.640
you will either read a text

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.520
and then answer questions about them

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:50.910
or you might hear something

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:52.280
and then you need to answer questions

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:54.960
or you might also need
to speak in a microphone

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.480
which would then be recorded
and rated afterwards.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:58.940
And of course you also might need

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.210
to write something in the writing section.

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:03.820
The different formats on
which the TOEFL has offered

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:06.970
the by far most common
ones, the TOEFL iBT.

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:09.220
So the TOEFL internet-Based Test.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:12.210
It pretty much means that you
need to go to a test center

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:14.940
where you then sit the test on a PC there

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:16.670
and then you get the result later on.

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:19.590
So what score is competitive on the TOEFL?

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:22.560
What score will you need to
attend a good university?

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.470
And here the range can be quite wide.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.420
If you already have university in mind

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:27.370
where you want to apply,

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:29.780
I encourage you to check
out their admissions page

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:31.630
because often they give you an indication

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:33.810
of what type of score they're looking for.

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:35.733
Sometimes they even
give you a minimum score

00:03:35.733 --> 00:03:38.980
that you need to get
an order to be eligible

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:40.830
to start studying at this place.

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:43.030
From doing my research when I was looking

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:45.520
for master programs and later also MBAs,

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.790
I concluded the most competitive programs

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:49.510
have a recommended minimum score

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:51.847
of something between a 100 and 105.

00:03:51.847 --> 00:03:55.110
So if you look at the Financial
Times, MBA ranking 2020,

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:57.800
the school that I attended
INSEAD is on number four.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:59.830
And on their page, you find information

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:01.980
that the minimum score that is recommended

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.260
is a TOEFL result of 105.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:06.100
And indeed the highest
core that I've found,

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:07.900
in my preparation is the the score

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:10.510
of Harvard Business
School of its MBA program.

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:12.930
It is the program that is
currently ranked number one

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:14.730
in the latest Financial Times ranking.

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:15.563
And they are telling you

00:04:15.563 --> 00:04:19.120
that their recommended
minimum score is 109.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:21.870
This is indeed the highest
number that I've seen out there.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:24.150
But let me know in the
comments if you know a school

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:25.920
which even post a higher number than that.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:27.810
So let's move to the next part.

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.900
So if you have the choice,
should you do the TOEFL

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:32.670
or its biggest competitor, the IELTS test.

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:36.590
Indeed truth is that these two
tests are not too different.

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:38.527
So probably you will be fine with both,

00:04:38.527 --> 00:04:40.470
but of course if you have the choice

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:43.200
and maybe also test center
for both of them nearby

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:44.910
you might of course ask
yourself the question,

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:46.400
well, which should I take?

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:48.720
So let me offer a couple of considerations

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:49.670
for you to think about.

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:51.180
So first of all you need to understand

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.320
the different origins of these tests.

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:55.980
So the IELTS is rather coming from the UK

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:58.540
and the TOEFL is rather
coming from the U.S.

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:02.350
So you might find some U.S.
schools more liking the TOEFL

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:04.400
and some U.K. more liking the IELTS

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.680
up to the point that maybe some
do not accept the other one.

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:09.410
Though probably, at least what I've seen,

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:12.920
almost all schools
accept both tests today.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:14.530
So this shouldn't be a huge issue

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:17.190
but if already have specific
universities in mind,

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:18.880
make sure that the test that you're taking

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:21.120
is actually accepted by the university.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.710
The next important consideration is

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:25.010
that the TOEFL test is much heavier

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:27.940
on multiple choice questions
that the IELTS test.

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:29.590
In the IELTS, they have
many more questions

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:32.350
where you have little open text fields

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:34.710
you can then write or type in answers

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:35.890
whereas in the TOEFL test

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:38.560
the share of multiple choice
questions is much higher.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:41.090
So if you have a preference
for the one over the other

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:43.070
this might be something
you want to think about.

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:45.450
The next is the way how
the test is conducted.

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:48.520
So for the TOEFL you
pretty much only can take

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:51.810
the iBT version, which is a
computer-based test version

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:53.470
where the need to go to a test center

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:55.360
and set the test in front of a PC.

00:05:55.360 --> 00:05:57.950
And this type exists
for the IELTS as well.

00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:00.040
But they are the paper-based tests

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:01.620
where it's more pen and paper-based.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:02.720
It's much more common.

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:04.530
So you will have much more opportunities

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:07.140
to take the IELTS test
in pen and paper format.

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:09.240
So if you do have a
preference for this type

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:11.320
of test-taking then the IELTS might indeed

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:12.580
be a great choice for you.

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:15.520
Finally and probably the
most significant difference

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:16.890
is the speaking part.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.350
So the way how the
speaking part is conducted

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:22.010
in the TOEFL test is that
you will have a microphone

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:23.130
and then you get a question

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:24.980
and then you will record your answer

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:26.680
by speaking to the microphone,

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:28.320
and then your answer will be recorded.

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.420
And afterwards it is rated by other people

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:32.880
who would listen to these
pre-recorded answers.

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:34.510
This is different to the IELTS test

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:36.320
but you will actually speak live

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:38.540
to another person that
you will sit in front of.

00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:41.010
So maybe you prefer
talking to a real person

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:42.860
but maybe you might also face the risk

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:44.560
that now sitting in front of such person

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:46.730
puts you off a little
bit, maybe irritates you,

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:48.430
the reaction that you get from the person.

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:50.510
So you also think about
how these situations

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:52.140
might evolve for yourself.

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:53.450
And then this might also be something

00:06:53.450 --> 00:06:55.520
that might guide you in
one direction or the other.

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:57.040
So let's move to the section

00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:58.480
where I want to share some tips

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:01.040
and reflections that I
believe will help you based

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:03.230
on my experiences with the TOEFL test.

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:06.390
And first of all, especially
if you compare it to the GMAT

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:08.210
you need to understand that the extent

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:10.890
to which you can
influence the TOEFL result

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:13.710
by your preparation is of course not zero

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:16.640
but it's not as large as the GMAT

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:17.870
or to put it differently,

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:19.730
if you're really good at speaking English

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:22.860
you will probably score
very high on the TOEFL

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:24.720
and if you're really bad at English

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:27.320
it'd be really difficult
for you to score high.

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:28.980
It doesn't matter how much you prepare.

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:31.110
This is of course different than the GMAT

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:33.600
because in the GMAT, even if you are smart

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:36.330
you will need to prepare,
it is the most cases.

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:38.000
So what this means for you is that

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.040
if you really want to
score high on the TOEFL

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:42.800
and especially if you have
a bit more time to prepare

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:44.920
maybe you know that in one or two years

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:46.440
you want to study abroad

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:48.240
and you need the TOEFL when you do that,

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:51.920
try to improve your intrinsic
English speaking abilities

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:54.677
because those in the end will
help you most in the test.

00:07:54.677 --> 00:07:57.320
And of course, the best way to
do that is to expose yourself

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:59.920
to situations where you
need to speak English.

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:01.310
So maybe you have the opportunity

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:03.100
to still do an internship abroad

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:05.460
before you do that semester
or that year abroad.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:07.330
So try to look for these opportunities

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:09.540
because those of course
will help tremendously.

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:12.260
Besides that, try to read
some English speaking magazine

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:13.740
some English speaking texts.

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:15.410
For instance, for business students,

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:18.320
magazines like The Economist
is of course a great choice

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:20.970
because indeed in the
TOEFL you will get exposed

00:08:20.970 --> 00:08:23.960
to more academic type of
texts in the reading section.

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:25.710
So getting used to reading these type

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:27.080
of texts will benefit you.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:29.420
And last but not least,
whenever you watch a video

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:31.500
on the streaming platform of your choice

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:34.070
for instance, Netflix,
try to watch it in English

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:36.050
maybe also activate English captions

00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:37.640
and then this way as well help you

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:39.950
to get the English
language into your head.

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:43.260
The next tip is to do not
worry about your accent.

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:45.060
So you'll find it in the documentation

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:47.320
the specific policy of the TOEFL

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:50.040
to not rate people by their accent.

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:51.330
So I know people who said,

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:54.950
oh shoot, now I need to try
to get rid of my German accent

00:08:54.950 --> 00:08:57.240
or my Italian accent or my Indian accent

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:59.590
because otherwise I might
score bad on the test.

00:08:59.590 --> 00:09:02.740
No guys, just make sure that
you can be easily understood

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:04.860
but the fact alone that you have an accent

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:07.610
will not lead to any point
reductions for yourself.

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:09.940
And indeed from reading
my YouTube comments

00:09:09.940 --> 00:09:12.160
I know that apparently many of you believe

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:13.850
that my English is horrible.

00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:16.550
And that I have a horrible German accent

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:18.010
and probably you are right.

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:21.690
But the truth is that for the
TOEFL, it's not important.

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:23.780
You will not get pointed
deduction for that.

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:25.660
Next, these books, and here, again,

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:28.670
contrary to the GMAT, I do not believe

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:31.270
that you will benefit now
from buying five books

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:32.670
and really working through them

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:34.430
just to score high on the tougher test.

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:36.810
Personally, I only worked
with your official book.

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:39.247
It is called The Official
Guide to the TOEFL Test

00:09:39.247 --> 00:09:41.390
and it is brought out by ETS,

00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:43.780
the official TOEFL test makers.

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:45.630
It has lots of practice questions,

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:48.350
lots of strategies on what
you can do on the exam.

00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:50.110
And it even has some mock tests.

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:51.960
So for me, that was more than enough

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:54.230
to feel fully prepared for the test.

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:56.040
If you're interested in
that, I will leave a link

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:58.100
to the book below in
the video description.

00:09:58.100 --> 00:09:59.170
In the end, what I believe,

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:01.400
what's just important
is that you are aware

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:03.490
what the test format will look like

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:06.390
That you're not surprised
how the menus look like

00:10:06.390 --> 00:10:08.560
once you sit in front for
the PC and take the test

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:11.410
and this book for me completely
fulfill this requirement.

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:13.300
It shows you what the
interface will look like

00:10:13.300 --> 00:10:14.980
and talks about what the format would be,

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:17.320
it provide some basic test questions.

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:19.680
So these for preparations
are absolutely fine.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:21.620
But of course, if you
do feel like you need

00:10:21.620 --> 00:10:23.740
a little bit more test practice,

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:25.630
just pick up maybe one or two more books.

00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:27.300
If you feel like that,
this is what you need,

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:29.430
just go on Amazon search for TOEFL books

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:31.420
and you will have lots of recommendations

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:33.210
and lists of books that you can look into.

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:34.770
Next, the practice test.

00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:37.640
So do recommend to do at
least one practice test

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:40.580
before the exam, just in deed
to get into the practice,

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:42.550
to know what you can expect.

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:44.640
And as I've just told you,
if you get the official book

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:47.780
then there are practice
tests included in there.

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:50.490
So you can just pick one of
those out, just go over it

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:52.380
just to get familiar with the format.

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:54.940
This is indeed something
that I would recommend to you

00:10:54.940 --> 00:10:56.580
in order, just to not be surprised

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:59.740
on the actual test day,
that it's not the first time

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:00.970
that you're seeing these things

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:02.460
when you walk into the test center.

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:03.860
My next advice would be that

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:06.640
you should invest a little bit of practice

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:08.430
into the speaking section.

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:09.560
And the point here is that

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:11.450
the TOEFL format is the bit special

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:14.580
in the sense that after you
are prompted with the question

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:18.890
you get 15 to 30 seconds
to prepare your answer.

00:11:18.890 --> 00:11:21.960
And then you get 45 to 60 seconds

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:25.160
to talk your answer into a microphone,

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:26.980
which then will be recorded.

00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:28.990
So pretty much what
you're required to do is

00:11:28.990 --> 00:11:31.680
in these 45 to 60 seconds

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:33.680
provide an answer to the question.

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:35.140
And of course you want to make sure

00:11:35.140 --> 00:11:37.060
that your answer is concise,

00:11:37.060 --> 00:11:38.840
that your answer is eloquent

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:40.280
that not in the end of the time,

00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:42.033
you suddenly realize that
you're running out of time,

00:11:42.033 --> 00:11:44.500
then are not able to
really finish the argument

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:45.510
that you wanted to do.

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:47.640
And believe me, this can easily happen

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:48.900
if you do not practice it.

00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:51.360
So familiarize yourself
with the type of questions

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:52.193
that you will get

00:11:52.193 --> 00:11:54.550
and then practice at home giving answers

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:57.690
in this time range of 45 to 60 seconds.

00:11:57.690 --> 00:11:59.490
Ask yourself, okay how many points

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:02.110
can I bring across in this time period?

00:12:02.110 --> 00:12:04.330
So the question might
for instance be, okay

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:06.520
talk about your favorite restaurant

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:09.190
and then talk about why this
is your favorite restaurant.

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:10.208
And then the question is, okay,

00:12:10.208 --> 00:12:12.000
when you give one reason, two reasons,

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:13.570
three reasons, four reasons

00:12:13.570 --> 00:12:14.910
and practice that in advance.

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:18.200
So what number of reasons,
what number of arguments

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:21.620
to questions like this, can
you provide in this time span?

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:22.610
And then you already know

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:23.830
that if you get such a question,

00:12:23.830 --> 00:12:25.300
you know that you can pretty much give

00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:27.390
two arguments, two reasons

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:29.170
and then you can comfortably provide

00:12:29.170 --> 00:12:30.640
these reasons in the question.

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:32.590
For me this really made a huge difference

00:12:32.590 --> 00:12:34.550
to practice this, to try this out at home.

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:36.910
And I can very much encourage
you to do the same thing.

00:12:36.910 --> 00:12:38.410
And the last point is to be aware

00:12:38.410 --> 00:12:41.110
that the TOEFL is very
heavy on multiple choice.

00:12:41.110 --> 00:12:42.850
So the good thing is that you can apply

00:12:42.850 --> 00:12:45.400
all the best practices
that exist out there

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:47.710
regarding multiple choice test questions.

00:12:47.710 --> 00:12:49.833
I could probably make a
whole video out of this.

00:12:49.833 --> 00:12:51.790
I think the most important thing to do

00:12:51.790 --> 00:12:54.380
is to pretty much make a
grid on your piece of paper.

00:12:54.380 --> 00:12:56.140
Make a grid for A B C D.

00:12:56.140 --> 00:12:58.060
So the four-answer options that you have.

00:12:58.060 --> 00:13:00.340
And then whenever you
go over the questions,

00:13:00.340 --> 00:13:02.110
you make columns for each questions

00:13:02.110 --> 00:13:03.610
and then you can use this grid

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:06.960
to cross out answer options
that you already know

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:09.150
and the process identifies
that they're not valid.

00:13:09.150 --> 00:13:10.620
Because if you do not do this,

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:12.030
what easily and often happens,

00:13:12.030 --> 00:13:13.730
if you have these four options

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:14.730
and then may be in the beginning

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:16.460
you read the first option, option A

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:18.430
and then you say, ah,
no, no, that's not it.

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:19.880
You pretty much excluded

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:21.757
but it will not make any
mark, just exclude it

00:13:21.757 --> 00:13:24.100
the you move to option B, C, and D

00:13:24.100 --> 00:13:25.540
and then probably you ponder,

00:13:25.540 --> 00:13:27.950
whether it's option B or option D

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:29.040
but then you're not sure.

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:30.700
And then you go back to option A

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:32.010
and then you try to remind yourself,

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:33.630
Oh, why was an option A

00:13:33.630 --> 00:13:35.850
and then you're very easily get confused.

00:13:35.850 --> 00:13:37.610
And then to rethink about it

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:40.020
even though you're pretty much
already excluded option A.

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:42.780
So if you're strictly
just cross out the answers

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:43.980
which are not applicable,

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:45.030
where you already know
that this is not it,

00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:47.800
then you will often also by elimination,

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:50.100
you can come to the finer answer

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:52.420
that then must be it even though positive

00:13:52.420 --> 00:13:54.840
you're not absolutely sure
that this is the best answer

00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:57.050
and indeed it often
helps it not get confused

00:13:57.050 --> 00:13:59.210
to really remind yourself
of what you've eliminated

00:13:59.210 --> 00:14:00.800
to then help you to dig deep, further

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:02.930
on the answers that are
still an option for you.

00:14:02.930 --> 00:14:04.590
So if you do follow this advice,

00:14:04.590 --> 00:14:06.640
I trust that your TOEFL test can be great,

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:08.910
that you will have great
chances to score high

00:14:08.910 --> 00:14:09.743
on the TOEFL test.

00:14:09.743 --> 00:14:11.030
And, of course, as you being,

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:13.790
